chapter = '------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# CHAPTER 237\n\n## THE BEARER\n\nThe pages were still on the table when I woke.\n\nThat was useful because I had put them there for exactly that reason.\n\nThe brown cloth was also still near the wall.\n\nThree pins.\n\nSame fold.\n\nMy damaged shirt remained on the shelf with three loose threads.\n\nLyssa was gone.\n\nNo beans.\n\nApparently tomorrow had kept them.\n\nI did not investigate.\n\nI read The Missing Key again while eating bread.\n\nEight lines.\n\nThree entrances.\n\nMessenger.\n\nThe second entrance still came sooner than my body wanted it to. The first was simple. Deliver a note. Leave.\n\nThe second came after barely half a page.\n\nThe third had enough room before it that I could forget I existed.\n\nDangerous structure.\n\nI read the second cue twice, folded the pages, and left before I could turn eight lines into a military campaign.\n\nThe morning was cool and dry. Carrow had already filled the lower streets with carts, baskets, shouting, and one man carrying a chair on his head because civilization was flexible.\n\nMy neck was fine.\n\nShoulders normal.\n\nRight hand normal.\n\nResidual limb comfortable.\n\nLeft crutch tip boring.\n\nGood.\n\nThe theatre side door opened cleanly when I pushed it.\n\nI stopped.\n\nClosed it.\n\nOpened it again.\n\nNo catch.\n\nJori, somewhere behind the stage, said, "Yes."\n\nI looked into the room.\n\n"Was that for me?"\n\n"The latch."\n\n"Fixed?"\n\nDavin appeared in the rear passage with a small file in one hand.\n\n"Mostly me."\n\nJori said, "Mostly the file."\n\nDavin looked at the file.\n\n"Then pay it."\n\nI went inside.\n\nThe narrow door frame from yesterday now stood onstage with an actual door hanging in it. The door had been painted a tired brown that looked respectable from ten feet away and diseased from three.\n\nNessa was at the property table with three folded notes, a brass-colored key, and the short dark-green coat.\n\nShe pointed at the coat.\n\n"On."\n\n"Good morning."\n\n"Coat."\n\n"Warmer."\n\nShe looked at me until I put my pages down.\n\nThe coat fit across my shoulders better than the black Magistrate coat had. Short hem. Clear of the crutches. Deep inside pocket on the right.\n\nI put one hand into it.\n\nNessa slapped the outside of the pocket.\n\n"Not yours."\n\n"I was checking."\n\n"You found it."\n\nShe handed me the first folded note.\n\n"One."\n\nThen the second.\n\n"Two."\n\nThen the third.\n\n"Three."\n\nThey were not identical.\n\nThe first was folded once.\n\nThe second twice.\n\nThe third had one corner tucked under.\n\nI looked at her.\n\n"Useful."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Did you do that for me?"\n\n"No. I did it for the play."\n\nFair.\n\nI put them into the inside pocket in order.\n\nMarek came through the stage door wearing a pale vest and carrying the brass-colored key.\n\nNessa held out her hand.\n\nHe stopped.\n\n"I just got here."\n\n"Key."\n\n"I need it."\n\n"Later."\n\n"It is called The Missing Key."\n\n"And this one is not missing yet."\n\nHe gave it to her.\n\nHealthy system.\n\nHara sat at the edge of the stage tying the repaired shoe tighter. The front no longer opened when she flexed it.\n\n"Comfortable?" I asked.\n\n"No."\n\n"Still attached?"\n\n"Unfortunately."\n\nPell was reading near the first row.\n\nTeren came in from the house aisle with his own pages.\n\n"Places."\n\nNo speech.\n\nNo encouragement.\n\nThe profession remained efficient.\n\nThe Missing Key was worse than I had understood at home.\n\nThis was praise.\n\nHara played the Mistress of the house.\n\nMarek played her younger brother, who had borrowed the key to a locked writing cabinet and then denied borrowing anything.\n\nPell played a family clerk who kept an inventory of objects nobody else respected.\n\nMy Messenger worked for someone who was not in the play and had apparently spent the entire day carrying increasingly unhelpful notes to the same address.\n\nThe missing key mattered because the Mistress needed a paper from the cabinet before an aunt arrived.\n\nThe aunt never appeared.\n\nThe paper might not have mattered.\n\nNobody knew.\n\nThe cabinet was represented by a narrow painted box on the side table.\n\nThe first note said the absent master had taken the key with him.\n\nThe second said he had sent it back yesterday.\n\nThe third said it was under the blue vase.\n\nThere was no blue vase onstage.\n\nThere had been.\n\nMarek\'s character had broken it before the play began.\n\nExcellent construction.\n\nWe started with entrances.\n\nI waited outside the brown door frame with the first note in my pocket.\n\nHara and Marek argued about the cabinet.\n\nPell read from the inventory.\n\nHara said, "Then where is the key?"\n\nI entered.\n\nTeren said, "No."\n\nI stopped.\n\n"I have a message."\n\n"You have a door."\n\nI looked at the door.\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Use it."\n\n"I did."\n\n"You entered like the wall had failed."\n\nI looked back at the frame.\n\nThe door was still swinging.\n\nApparently I had pushed harder than necessary.\n\nAgain.\n\nHara: "Then where is the key?"\n\nI opened the door.\n\nToo slowly.\n\nTeren said, "No."\n\nI looked at him.\n\n"Strong range."\n\n"You\'re deciding the entrance matters."\n\n"I am entering."\n\n"Correct."\n\nThat was not helpful.\n\nHara said, "He means stop presenting the entrance."\n\nTeren looked at her.\n\nShe shrugged.\n\n"Did I improve it?"\n\n"Unfortunately."\n\nAgain.\n\nThis time I opened the door and came through it because I needed to be on the other side.\n\nBetter.\n\nI crossed toward Hara.\n\nThe easiest route took me in front of Pell.\n\nJori said, "Back."\n\nI backed up.\n\nHe pointed at the space behind Pell\'s chair.\n\n"Through there."\n\nThe lane was wide enough if I kept the right crutch close.\n\nI tried it.\n\nClear.\n\nNo chair.\n\nNo coat hem.\n\nNo foot.\n\n"Keep," Teren said.\n\nNessa put a chalk nick near the door side of the route.\n\nNot a speech.\n\nA place.\n\nAgain.\n\nI entered, used the lane, stopped beside Hara, and held out the first note.\n\n"Message for this house."\n\nHara took it.\n\nI waited.\n\nShe opened it.\n\nRead.\n\nLooked at Marek.\n\nI waited more.\n\nTeren said, "Why are you still there?"\n\n"I want to know what it says."\n\n"Greg does."\n\n"Messenger also might."\n\n"Messenger has work."\n\nI looked at the door.\n\n"Exit."\n\n"Yes."\n\nI left.\n\nThat was the correction.\n\nNot mysterious.\n\nDeliver.\n\nLeave.\n\nThe second entrance was worse because the scene had barely recovered from the first note.\n\nMarek insisted the absent master had the key.\n\nPell found a receipt showing the key had been sent.\n\nHara asked who had brought the receipt.\n\nPell said, "A messenger."\n\nI entered.\n\nTeren said, "No."\n\nI stopped in the doorway.\n\n"That is literally me."\n\n"That is not your cue."\n\nI looked at the page.\n\nMy cue came two lines later.\n\nMarek had to say, "Then find the fool who carried it."\n\nI closed the door.\n\nFrom the stage Hara said, "Convenient."\n\n"Hostile workplace."\n\nAgain.\n\nThis time I waited through "A messenger."\n\nWaited through Hara asking which messenger.\n\nWaited through Marek saying he did not care.\n\nThen:\n\n"Then find the fool who carried it."\n\nI opened the door.\n\n"Again?"\n\nSmall laugh from Hara.\n\nNot audience.\n\nJust Hara.\n\nTeren let it stand.\n\nI handed the second note to Pell.\n\nThen I stayed to see him open it.\n\nTeren pointed at the door.\n\nRight.\n\nI left.\n\nThird entrance.\n\nBy then the brother had admitted he might have touched the key.\n\nThe Clerk had established that touching and borrowing were different entries in the household accounts.\n\nHara was considering murder.\n\nMy third note came after a longer exchange.\n\nI almost relaxed too much.\n\nNessa caught me reading over Marek\'s shoulder from offstage.\n\n"Ready."\n\n"I am."\n\n"You\'re not."\n\nShe tapped my pocket.\n\nI had put the third note in the wrong inside fold after the second pass.\n\nI checked.\n\nFirst note gone.\n\nSecond note gone.\n\nThird note had slid flat under my pages.\n\nI fixed it.\n\n"Thank you."\n\n"Pocket."\n\nThere were relationships built entirely from nouns.\n\nWe ran it again.\n\nThen again.\n\nThe door stopped being an event.\n\nThe notes stayed in order.\n\nI left after delivering them.\n\nMy second entrance waited for the actual cue instead of the word messenger.\n\nBy the fourth pass, Teren stopped stopping me.\n\nThis felt suspicious.\n\nAt midday, Rinna came in with bread, two apples, and no new information.\n\nI knew that because nobody asked.\n\nShe set the food down.\n\nMarek reached for an apple.\n\nRinna slapped his hand with a folded handbill.\n\n"Paid performers first."\n\n"I am a performer."\n\n"Not paid yet."\n\n"Technical distinction."\n\nHara took the better apple.\n\nOf course.\n\nI sat on the stage edge to rest my right leg.\n\nOrdinary stiffness.\n\nShoulders worked.\n\nRight palm warm from the crutches.\n\nNothing concerning.\n\nPell sat one seat below me.\n\n"Messenger."\n\n"Clerk."\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe had communicated fully.\n\nRinna looked at Teren.\n\n"Keep it?"\n\n"Maybe."\n\nI looked at the pages.\n\n"Performance?"\n\nTeren looked at me.\n\n"Eat."\n\nClear.\n\nWe ate.\n\nNobody said brown coat.\n\nNobody said lamp glass.\n\nNobody said front door or back door except Davin, who came through the repaired rear passage and told Jori the back door now shut properly.\n\nJori went to test it.\n\nIt did.\n\nThey moved on to the next problem.\n\nBy early afternoon, Teren had decided.\n\n"The Missing Key stays."\n\nMarek raised both hands.\n\n"Justice."\n\nTeren ignored him.\n\nRinna looked at me.\n\n"Messenger."\n\n"I remain."\n\n"Today."\n\n"Yes."\n\nNessa took the dark-green coat off me, brushed chalk from one sleeve, and handed it back.\n\nApparently that had been a reset.\n\nThe first piece was one Greg was not in.\n\nGood.\n\nI spent it backstage with the coat on a peg and the three message props on Nessa\'s table.\n\nMarek was in the first piece too.\n\nHe came off after one scene sweating under the pale vest.\n\n"Audience good."\n\n"How many?"\n\n"Audience."\n\n"Useful."\n\n"They laughed."\n\n"At you?"\n\n"Near me."\n\nMore honest than expected.\n\nHara came through behind him.\n\n"Not at him."\n\nThere it was.\n\nWhen The Missing Key came up, the set changed quickly.\n\nBrown door frame.\n\nTwo chairs.\n\nSmall table.\n\nPainted cabinet.\n\nNo blue vase.\n\nThe missing vase was represented by its absence, which was economical.\n\nNessa placed the three notes in my coat pocket herself.\n\n"One. Two. Three."\n\n"I know."\n\n"I know you know."\n\nThis was somehow worse.\n\nThe house quieted.\n\nHara and Marek began.\n\nFrom offstage, I could hear the play find its shape.\n\nMarek was better with an audience.\n\nNot because he became smarter.\n\nBecause his denial grew more confident every time the room doubted him.\n\nPell entered with the inventory book.\n\nHara asked where the key was.\n\nMy first cue.\n\nI opened the door.\n\nNot important.\n\nJust a door.\n\nCross behind Pell.\n\nStop.\n\n"Message for this house."\n\nHara took the note.\n\nI left.\n\nThe audience barely noticed me.\n\nGood.\n\nBehind the flat, Nessa held up two fingers.\n\nSecond note.\n\nI put my hand into the pocket and found the double fold.\n\nThe scene moved faster than rehearsal.\n\nThat was the first problem.\n\nPell said, "A messenger."\n\nMy body prepared.\n\nNot cue.\n\nHara asked which messenger.\n\nMarek said he did not care.\n\n"Then find the fool who carried it."\n\nI opened the door.\n\n"Again?"\n\nThe audience laughed.\n\nMore than Hara had.\n\nNot a lot.\n\nEnough to make the next beat feel longer.\n\nI crossed.\n\nHanded Pell the note.\n\nThen made my mistake.\n\nI stayed.\n\nOne beat.\n\nTwo.\n\nI wanted to hear the laugh settle.\n\nI wanted to see Pell open the note.\n\nI had spent the whole morning being told not to do exactly this.\n\nTeren was not visible.\n\nHe did not need to be.\n\nHara looked at me.\n\nNot Greg.\n\nMessenger.\n\nShe held out one hand.\n\n"Unless you\'ve brought the key too?"\n\nWritten line.\n\nMy exit was supposed to happen before it.\n\nBecause I was still there, the question landed directly on me.\n\nI said my next written line too early.\n\n"No key."\n\nThat line belonged to the third entrance.\n\nPell looked at me.\n\nMarek looked at me.\n\nI knew immediately.\n\nThe audience did not.\n\nHara did.\n\nShe looked at my empty hand.\n\nThen at Pell\'s note.\n\nThen said, "Wonderful. We have acquired a messenger who spoils tomorrow."\n\nThe room laughed.\n\nNot a giant explosion.\n\nA good one.\n\nShe turned away from me.\n\nThat was my exit.\n\nI took it.\n\nBackstage, my face was hot.\n\nNessa held out one hand.\n\nI stared at her.\n\n"Third note."\n\nRight.\n\nI took it from my pocket and gave it to her.\n\n"No."\n\nShe put it back into my hand.\n\n"Keep it."\n\n"I just used the line."\n\n"Not the note."\n\nCorrect.\n\nThe scene continued.\n\nI had created a problem.\n\nHara had continued.\n\nImportant distinction.\n\nI stood by the door and listened.\n\nPell read the second note.\n\nThe absent master claimed he had sent the key yesterday.\n\nMarek\'s brother said that proved nothing.\n\nHara asked what exactly would prove something.\n\nThe audience laughed before the next line because they had learned him.\n\nGood.\n\nMy third cue approached.\n\nI had already spoken "No key."\n\nThe third entrance still existed.\n\nThe note still existed.\n\nThe remaining lines still existed.\n\nI did not invent a replacement.\n\nMarek admitted that there had once been a blue vase.\n\nHara became very still.\n\nPell checked the inventory.\n\n"Blue vase. Hall table."\n\nHara said, "Where is it?"\n\nMarek said, "Define is."\n\nBig laugh.\n\nMine came after.\n\nI opened the door.\n\nThird entrance.\n\nThe audience recognized me.\n\nA smaller laugh started before I spoke.\n\nI did not wait for it.\n\n"Last message."\n\nHara looked at me.\n\n"Does it contain the key?"\n\nThat was supposed to set up the line I had already used.\n\nNo key.\n\nI could not say it again without making the mistake visible.\n\nFor half a second, I had nothing.\n\nThen Pell took the note from my hand.\n\nNot a rescue flourish.\n\nHe simply took it because the Clerk had taken the previous one.\n\n"Perhaps we should read it."\n\nHis line was not written there.\n\nIt worked.\n\nHara turned to him.\n\nThe scene moved.\n\nI left.\n\nPell opened the third note and read the actual message.\n\n"The key is under the blue vase."\n\nSilence.\n\nHara looked toward the empty table.\n\nThen at Marek.\n\nMarek looked at the empty table too.\n\n"Define under."\n\nThe audience broke.\n\nHara did not hurry.\n\nShe walked to him.\n\nSlow.\n\nHeld out one hand.\n\nMarek reached into his vest and produced nothing.\n\nOther pocket.\n\nNothing.\n\nThen he looked toward Nessa\'s property table offstage as though the missing vase might be hiding there.\n\nHara said, "Which piece?"\n\nThat was written.\n\nIt owned the room.\n\nMarek\'s answer disappeared under the laugh.\n\nHe waited.\n\nGood.\n\nThen tried again.\n\nThe scene finished with Pell discovering the cabinet had never been locked.\n\nThe brass-colored key had belonged to the hall cupboard.\n\nHara opened the painted cabinet with one finger.\n\nLooked inside.\n\nLooked at Marek.\n\nClosed it.\n\nCurtain.\n\nThe audience gave us the kind of applause a short stupid play deserved.\n\nWarm.\n\nNot immortal.\n\nEnough.\n\nBackstage, I took off the green coat.\n\nNessa checked the pocket before taking it.\n\nAll three notes accounted for.\n\n"Good," she said.\n\nI looked at her.\n\n"Don\'t."\n\nShe almost smiled.\n\nTeren found me while the others were resetting.\n\n"You stayed."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Why?"\n\n"The laugh."\n\n"Yours?"\n\n"Partly."\n\n"Then leave with it."\n\nI nodded.\n\nThat was specific enough to hurt.\n\n"I also used the third line."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Pell continued."\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Hara too."\n\n"Yes."\n\nI waited.\n\nTeren looked at me.\n\n"Again tomorrow if it stays paired."\n\nNot praise.\n\nNot punishment.\n\nA work condition.\n\n"Same role?"\n\n"Unless I change it."\n\n"Useful."\n\n"Don\'t improve it."\n\nThere he was.\n\nRinna paid me one copper.\n\nI put it away.\n\nNo Vale calculation.\n\nNo threat calculation.\n\nMoney.\n\nThe back latch closed cleanly when people began leaving.\n\nI noticed because yesterday it had not.\n\nThat was all.\n\nHara sat on the stage edge retightening the repaired shoe.\n\nI stopped beside her.\n\n"Thank you."\n\n"For what?"\n\n"Continuing."\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nThen at Teren across the room.\n\n"Did he make you say that?"\n\n"No."\n\n"Then you\'re getting strange."\n\n"Already happened."\n\nShe nodded.\n\n"Fair."\n\nMarek appeared with the brass-colored key.\n\nNessa appeared behind him.\n\nHe put it down without being asked.\n\nGrowth.\n\nPossibly fear.\n\nI left before anyone found more work.\n\nAt home, Lyssa was already there.\n\nThe brown cloth was still near the wall.\n\nThree pins.\n\nSame fold.\n\nMy damaged shirt still had three loose threads.\n\nLyssa had a bowl in front of her and something wrapped in paper near her elbow.\n\nNot beans.\n\nI looked at it.\n\nShe looked at me looking.\n\n"No."\n\n"I didn\'t ask."\n\n"You were going to."\n\n"Maybe."\n\nShe ate.\n\nI put The Missing Key pages on the table.\n\n"Show?"\n\n"Yes."\n\n"Good?"\n\nI took bread.\n\n"Hara was."\n\nLyssa nodded as if this confirmed an existing law.\n\n"I was useful."\n\n"Less good."\n\n"Relationship damaged."\n\nShe ate another spoonful.\n\nI sat across from her.\n\nNo report about lamp glass.\n\nNo analysis of doors.\n\nNo magic.\n\nNo Vale.\n\nAfter a minute she pointed at the pages.\n\n"Tomorrow?"\n\n"Maybe."\n\n"Then don\'t lose them."\n\nI looked at the title.\n\nThe Missing Key.\n\nI looked back at her.\n\nShe had already gone back to eating.\n\nI kept the pages where I could see them.\n'

